Exercise 2.3 - Read the Brewery’s Order File
The order file of a brewery lands on your desk. The sales team exported it from a German spreadsheet: columns are separated by ;, and numbers use a decimal comma, like 60,0. The following code writes the file to ExampleData/orders.csv exactly as you received it:

mkpath("$(@__DIR__)/ExampleData")
write("$(@__DIR__)/ExampleData/orders.csv", """
week;crates;note
1;53;
2;60,0;
3;61;
4;;
5;56;
6;126;harbour festival
7;60;
7;60;
8;59;
""")
println("File 'orders.csv' written to the folder ExampleData!")

First, read the file with plain CSV.read(path, DataFrame) and print it. What type does the crates column have, and why? Then read the file again into the variable orders, this time telling CSV.read() how the file was written: delim=';' and decimal=','.






Keyword arguments come after a semicolon: CSV.read(path, DataFrame; delim=';', decimal=','). Other tools treat the comma as a thousands separator and read 60,0 as 600.




# YOUR CODE BELOW



Code
# Test your answer
@assert orders.crates[2] == 60.0 "Week 2 should read 60.0 crates. Did you set decimal=','?"
@assert ismissing(orders.crates[4]) "Week 4 has no number in the file, so it should be missing."
println("Order file read successfully!")

Exercise 2.4 - Clean the Order File
Two problems remain: week 7 appears twice, and week 4 is missing. Remove the duplicated row with unique!(), then replace the missing value with the median of the other weeks. Week 6 looks unusual too, but read its note before you touch it.






median() comes with the Statistics package (using Statistics; it ships with Julia). skipmissing() ignores missing values, and coalesce.(column, value) replaces every missing entry with value.




# YOUR CODE BELOW



Code
# Test your answer
@assert nrow(orders) == 8 "Week 7 should appear only once. Did you remove the duplicated row?"
@assert count(ismissing, orders.crates) == 0 "There are still missing values in the crates column."
@assert orders.crates[4] == 60.0 "Week 4 should get the median of the other weeks, 60 crates."
@assert orders.crates[6] == 126 "The harbour festival in week 6 was real: keep its 126 crates."
println("Order file cleaned successfully!")

