Exercise 2.2 - Create a Matrix of Variables
Now, imagine you’re deciding whether to stock 4 different products in 3 different stores. Create a 3x4 matrix of binary variables called stock_decision.

# YOUR CODE BELOW



Code
# Test your answer
@assert size(stock_decision) == (3, 4)
@assert all(is_binary(stock_decision[i,j]) for i in 1:3, j in 1:4)
@assert all(is_valid(model, stock_decision[i,j]) for i in 1:3, j in 1:4)
println("Excellent! You've created a 3x4 matrix of binary variables for stocking decisions.")


Matrices of variables like this one are the backbone of many optimization models. For example, the transportation problem in Tutorial III.V uses exactly this shape to decide how much to ship from each supplier to each customer.

Exercise 3.1 - Set Bounds on a Variable
Create a variable temperature that represents the temperature setting on a thermostat. It should be between 0 and 37 degrees Celsius. The test checks both the lower and the upper bound, so set both at once using the interval syntax lower &lt;= variable &lt;= upper from the examples above.

